  "favorite": {
    "reasons": "The Odyssey is my favorite literary masterpiece because it combines an exciting adventure with a thoughtful view of what makes a journey meaningful. As someone who enjoys gaming and watching movies, I appreciate stories with difficult challenges, memorable characters, and choices that carry consequences. Homer's epic offers these qualities, but Odysseus's purpose gives them greater weight. He is trying to return to Ithaca and recover the relationships and sense of belonging disrupted by war and separation. His destination matters because of the life connected to it.\n\nI also appreciate Odysseus’s combination of resourcefulness and flaws. His intelligence helps him survive, yet pride and curiosity can place him and his companions in danger. This makes him interesting to follow and gives his choices real weight. It encourages me to admire determination while still questioning how a person uses talent and power. The epic's mixture of adventure, uncertainty, and responsibility gives me something to reflect on beyond the excitement of each encounter.",
    "influences": "The lesson that stands out most to me is the need to balance ability with self-control. In Book IX, Odysseus's clever escape from Polyphemus is followed by his decision to reveal his name, exposing how pride can undermine good judgment. The Sirens episode in Book XII offers another perspective: he prepares restraints and relies on his crew to enforce them. I find this significant because it presents accepting limits and listening to others as forms of strength. Intelligence becomes more valuable when it is guided by responsibility.\n\nPenelope also influences my appreciation of the epic. Her importance extends beyond waiting for Odysseus. In Book XXIII, her test involving their marriage bed shows caution, intelligence, and a need for trustworthy evidence before recognition. I admire the way this scene makes reunion depend on shared understanding rather than appearance alone. Together, these characters encourage me to value perseverance, thoughtful judgment, and relationships built on trust. For me, their reunion shows that coming home also means rebuilding understanding between people.",
    "connections": "As a fourth-year BSIT student, I connect the journey in The Odyssey with the continuing effort to find direction and prepare for life beyond college. Preparing for work in technology means learning continually, asking questions, and approaching problems with patience. A difficult step does not have to define the whole journey. Odysseus's experiences encourage me to focus on how I respond to challenges rather than expect a clear path at every moment. His mistakes also remind me that confidence should leave room for advice, correction, and a willingness to reconsider a decision.\n\nMy interest in games and movies gives me another connection to the story. I enjoy following characters through challenges, but this epic invites me to consider the judgment behind their actions and the people affected by them. I want to carry that habit of reflection into my own choices: think before acting, remain open to other perspectives, and remember the purpose behind a goal. For me, the journey home represents the effort to grow without losing sight of the values and relationships that make achievement worthwhile."
  },
  "learning": {
    "historical": "The readings in this portfolio have helped me see literature as a record of how different communities imagine a meaningful life. Homer’s Greek epics connect personal choices with honor, relationships, and the consequences of conflict. The opening chapters of The Tale of Genji reveal the courtly world of Heian Japan, shaped by rank and unequal freedom, while the medieval French poem The Song of Roland connects heroic identity with loyalty to a ruler and a religious community. These settings give familiar human concerns different forms.\n\nI have also come to appreciate the variety of literary expression. The biblical Psalms 23 and 91 communicate trust through concentrated poetic images, while the Sanskrit epics The Mahabharata and The Ramayana explore duty through extended narratives and difficult choices. Paying attention to each work’s cultural setting helps me interpret its values carefully and recognize the questions it continues to raise for readers today.",
    "lenses": "Using different literary lenses has shown me how a focused question can deepen interpretation. A moral and philosophical reading of The Odyssey examines the responsibility behind Odysseus’s cleverness, while a psychological reading of The Iliad explores how anger and grief shape Achilles’s conduct. Comparing the imagery and changing voices of Psalms 23 and 91 shows how a formalist approach connects a poem’s construction with its emotional effect.\n\nOther approaches make questions of power and tradition clearer. A feminist reading of Genji’s opening chapters considers women’s choices within court society. A historical reading of Roland distinguishes its legendary representation from the event behind it. The Mahabharata invites ethical questions about duty and compassion, while an archetypal reading of The Ramayana examines exile, trials, helpers, and return. Each lens directs attention toward particular evidence and possibilities of meaning.",
    "human": "Across these works, I find recurring concerns about belonging, suffering, loyalty, and the use of power. Odysseus’s longing for home gives achievement a personal purpose. Achilles’s meeting with Priam shows how recognizing another person’s grief can change an encounter between enemies. The Psalms express confidence through images of guidance and protection. Together, these readings encourage me to consider how people seek security and understanding in uncertain circumstances.\n\nLiterature also helps me question the effects of social expectations. The experiences of women in Genji, Draupadi’s challenge in The Mahabharata, and Sita’s responses in The Ramayana invite attention to whose voice is heard and whose suffering is accepted. I want to carry that attentiveness into my own judgments. Understanding another perspective requires patience, especially when a situation involves competing responsibilities or an unequal distribution of power.",
    "skills": "The work of interpretation has helped me connect an idea with the evidence needed to support it. A character’s decision, a repeated image, or a change in tone can become the starting point for an argument. I am learning to explain why a detail matters, consider another possible reading, and keep the text’s events distinct from my own response. Identifying editions and acknowledging sources also make an interpretation easier for another reader to assess.\n\nAs a BSIT student, I see a connection between these habits and responsible work in technology. Understanding a problem requires careful observation, clear reasoning, and attention to the people affected by a solution. That includes listening to users and explaining technical decisions in language others can understand. My interest in games and movies provides further opportunities to examine stories, motives, and consequences. Great Books gives me a more thoughtful way to approach those interests and a stronger foundation for communicating ideas with clarity and care."
  },
